{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01LjmMBmv7N1byNBse9N456y", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavo_Kingdom\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Lopburi\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayodhyapura\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nripatindravarman_III\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kesariraja\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ipoia_Sanne_Thora_Thesma_Teperat\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalavarnadisharaja\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siddhijaya_Brahmadeva\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.ancientamulet.com/?p=6825\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:20:22.007Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "Lavo Khmer Empire 10th century submitted called in Khmer \"Lavo\" Lvo Lavapura", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01P5ms4wtFr3n65sW9cZwzMj", "content": [{"title": "Lavo Kingdom", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavo_Kingdom"}, {"title": "History of Lopburi", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Lopburi"}, {"title": "Ayodhyapura", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayodhyapura"}, {"title": "Nripatindravarman III", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nripatindravarman_III"}, {"title": "Kesariraja", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kesariraja"}, {"title": "Ipoia Sanne Thora Thesma Teperat", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ipoia_Sanne_Thora_Thesma_Teperat"}, {"title": "Kalavarnadisharaja", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalavarnadisharaja"}, {"title": "Siddhijaya Brahmadeva", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siddhijaya_Brahmadeva"}, {"title": "Historical Foundations: From Mon-Dvaravati to Khmer Hegemony", "url": "https://www.ancientamulet.com/?p=6825"}]}, "The search turned up useful background on Lavo, but nothing that gives a Khmer-language name for it.\n\n- **Basic identity:** Lavo was a mandala in the lower Chao Phraya basin, with its centre at Lavapura (modern Lopburi) until the 1080s, when it moved to Ayodhya (Xiān) (1-2, 1-3).\n- **Khmer rule in the 10th century:** The Khmers were present from the tenth century, and Lavo's status as a subordinate of the Khmer empire dates from roughly the 10th to 16th centuries (2-7, 2-10). The infobox lists Lavo as an Angkor vassal from 946 to 1052, and gives 1001 as the date it was destroyed by Angkor (1-1).\n- **Conflicting dates:** One article suggests the relocation of Lavapura's centre came in the late 10th century, before Angkor sacked the city in 1005 (6-2). The 1001 and 1005 dates don't match, so treat both as unverified.\n- **Founding and early period:** Lavo was traditionally founded in 648 by Kalavarnadisharaja (7-3). Its early population is described as mainly ethnic Mon, practising Theravada Buddhism, within the Dvaravati mandala (9-3, 9-4).\n- **Later:** Lavo was merged into the Ayutthaya kingdom, founded in 1350 (2-11).\n\nThe sources I found don't give a Khmer-script or Khmer-language name for Lavo, so the \"called in Khmer\" part of your query is unresolved. A targeted search on Khmer inscriptions or Coedès' work on Lavo might settle it."], "durationSeconds": 6.679744042, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01LjmMBmv7N1byNBse9N456y", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
